            <ArticleTitle>Spinal brucellosis: case report in the United States.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E250-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To increase awareness of spinal brucellosis and discuss demographics, diagnosis, and treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Brucellosis is a rare cause of spinal infections in the United States, although there have been regional increases in its prevalence.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective review of a patient with spinal brucellosis. She underwent a protracted course of treatment, with a long delay in diagnosis. History ultimately revealed regular consumption of unpasteurized goat cheese. Appropriate testing subsequently led to the diagnosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After multiple surgeries and medications, the condition responded well to definitive antibiotic therapy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Brucellosis is rare in the United States and thus often overlooked in the differential diagnosis of back pain. The changing risk pattern for this disease requires a high index of suspicion, which can result in early diagnosis and predictably favorable results to treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
